{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Also, do not curse him.</b> He said <b>גַּם</b> (also), and doubled the expression, saying ״קֹב תִּקֳּבֶנּוּ״ — meaning, because Balaam had said to him ״קוּם בָּלָק וְגוֹ׳ הַאֲזִינָה״ וְגוֹ׳, and there he hinted to him that he wished to curse, but the angel turned his mouth, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Bemidbar Rabbah here) — therefore too he said ״גַּם קֹב״, meaning: not only shall you not curse them with explicit intention, but even to utter a curse from your mouth without mentioning their name — you shall not utter it. From this we see that Balaam the wicked was cunning, seeking to produce from his mouth a curse in a manner that the Creator would not punish him — in this way: that he would say ״אָרוּר״ (cursed) or ״מְקֻלָּל״ (execrated) without bringing forth from his mouth the name of the cursed or execrated one, but in his heart he would intend what he wished. And for this reason HaShem prevented him from uttering a curse from his mouth and sealed his heart." "And His saying <b>גַּם בָּרֵךְ</b> (also bless): because Balaam had told him that he does not bless them of his own will but is compelled to bless them, therefore he said to him: ״If you do not curse, you are not obligated to bless,״ and he thought that HaShem would not turn his mouth to bless unless he wished to curse. And he doubled the expression, saying <b>גַּם בָּרֵךְ לֹא תְבָרֲכֶנּוּ</b> — meaning, even a blessing that is not truly a blessing according to Balaam's mind and intention — even this you shall not pronounce over them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:32.114659554Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}